From Late {{inh|en|enm|hagis||haggis}}, from {{m|enm|hag}}, {{m|enm|haggen||to chop, cut, hack; to cut into}} (from {{der|en|non|hǫggva||to hew}}),<ref>{{R:Oxford Dictionaries Online|nodot=yes}}; {{R:MED Online|entry=haggen, v.|id=MED19773|accessdate=28 October 2017}}</ref> or from {{m|enm|hakken||to chop, hack; to dice, mince}} (from {{inh|en|ang|hēawan||to chop, hew; to dice, mince}}),<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=hakken, v.|id=MED19811|accessdate=28 October 2017}}</ref> both ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kewh₂-||to hew; to beat, strike; to forge}}.<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=hagis, n.|id=MED19777|accessdate=28 October 2017}}</ref>

====Pronunciation====
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈhæɡɪs/|lang=en}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈhæɡəs/|lang=en}}
* {{hyphenation|hag|gis|lang=en}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~|es}}

# A [[traditional]] [[Scottish]] [[dish#Noun|dish]] made from [[minced#Adjective|minced]] [[sheep]] [[offal]] with [[oatmeal]] and [[spice#Noun|spices]], etc., originally [[boil#Verb|boiled]] in the [[stomach#Noun|stomach]] of a sheep but now often in an [[artificial]] [[casing#Noun|casing]], and usually served with [[neeps]] and [[tatties]] ([[mashed#Adjective|mashed]] [[swede]] and [[potato]]es) and [[accompany|accompanied]] with [[whisky]].
#* {{quote-book|title=The Reverie: Or, A Flight to the Paradise of Fools|page=246|author=Charles Johnstone|year=1762|passage=— "An '''haggis''',"— said a Scot. — "Leek-pottage,"— sputtered Taffy. In a word, word, they all raised their voices with such vehemence, not one attending to what another said, that since the building of Babel, there was not such a scene of confusion.}}
#* {{quote-book|title=The American Pastry Cook|author=Jessup Whitehead|yea=1894|passage=Another one of those revered patriarchs whom it were almost sacrilege to touch is Burn's, with "honest sonsie face," the '''haggis'''.|year=1894}}

